S645 Perfect Numbers as Aliquot Fixed Points
============================================

Carrier
-------
divisor lattice D(n) -> sigma(n) -> aliquot map s(n)=sigma(n)-n
perfect means s(n)=n, equivalently sigma(n)=2n or abundancy A(n)=2
the side channel is the prime-power product for sigma(n)/n

Fixed points
------------
aliquot fixed points n<= 100000: [6, 28, 496, 8128]
  n=6     factors=2 * 3            tau=4  sigma=12     s(n)=6     A=2
  n=28    factors=2^2 * 7          tau=6  sigma=56     s(n)=28    A=2
  n=496   factors=2^4 * 31         tau=10 sigma=992    s(n)=496   A=2
  n=8128  factors=2^6 * 127        tau=14 sigma=16256  s(n)=8128  A=2

Euclid-Euler even section
-------------------------
  p=2  M=2^p-1=3        n=2^(p-1)M=6            in window
  p=3  M=2^p-1=7        n=2^(p-1)M=28           in window
  p=5  M=2^p-1=31       n=2^(p-1)M=496          in window
  p=7  M=2^p-1=127      n=2^(p-1)M=8128         in window
  p=13 M=2^p-1=8191     n=2^(p-1)M=33550336     beyond window
  p=17 M=2^p-1=131071   n=2^(p-1)M=8589869056   beyond window
  p=19 M=2^p-1=524287   n=2^(p-1)M=137438691328 beyond window
  p=31 M=2^p-1=2147483647 n=2^(p-1)M=2305843008139952128 beyond window
  identity: sigma(2^(p-1))*sigma(M) = (2^p-1)*2^p = 2n

Aliquot cycles in the finite window
-----------------------------------
  length 1: count=4, sample=[(6,), (28,), (496,), (8128,)]
  length 2: count=13, sample=[(220, 284), (1184, 1210), (2620, 2924), (5020, 5564), (6232, 6368), (10744, 10856), (12285, 14595), (17296, 18416)]
  length 5: count=1, sample=[(12496, 14288, 15472, 14536, 14264)]
  fixed points are length-1 cycles; amicable pairs are length-2 cycles.

